      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Inhaled corticosteroids (ICSs) are one of the suggested first-line therapies for patients with persistent asthma of moderate severity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The efficacy and safety of mometasone furoate (MF) 400 microg twice daily (BID) and fluticasone propionate (FP) 500 microg BID administered for 12 weeks via dry powder inhaler (DPI) were compared in a noninferiority trial, in adults with moderate-to-severe persistent asthma. The primary variable was the change from baseline in am peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR). PM PEFR, forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV(1)), asthma symptoms, rescue medication use, response to therapy, exacerbation rates, and adverse events were also assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The lower bound of 95% CIs for treatment differences in the primary variable ranged from 2.6% to 5.6% throughout the 12-week study and were within the prespecified noninferiority range. No significant between-group differences were observed in lung function, rescue medication use, response to therapy, exacerbation rates, or adverse events. At most of the weeks assessed, there were no between-group differences in asthma symptoms. Most adverse events were mild-to-moderate."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "MF-DPI 400 microg BID was therapeutically equivalent to FP-DPI 500 microg BID in patients with moderate-to-severe persistent asthma."
        }
      ]},
